// Judged suite — "was the reply any GOOD?" Spec §7.
//
// A separate suite with a separate blocking rule, because this is the least
// trustworthy number in the harness. It answers the question the deterministic
// scorers cannot, and it is the only scorer that reads the reply text at all.
//
// GATE: mean >= 3.5/5 AND no single case below 2/5. The floor exists because a
// mean hides catastrophes — one 0/5 disaster averages away behind four 5s and
// ships. Mean measures the suite; the floor measures the worst case, and for a
// release gate the worst case is the one that matters.
//
// A judge outage does NOT fail these cases. They report "skipped" and the gate
// stays open: a harness that goes red because a third party 429'd teaches the
// team to ignore red.
//
// Run separately, and AFTER the deterministic suites (spec §9.2 — "exit 1,
// judge not run"), which the shell gives you for free:
//   freecode eval trajectory --gate && freecode eval judged --gate
//
// Every case still carries trajectory expectations. They are cheap, they are
// objective, and a case whose agent wrote to disk should fail on that fact
// rather than on a grader's opinion of the prose.
{"id": "explain-a-module", "prompt": "In two or three sentences, what does apps/core/src/eval/gate.ts do and why does it not simply require every case to pass?", "rubric": "answer-quality", "expectMaxTurns": 5, "forbidTools": ["write", "edit"]}
